What each column means, because the shapes are not the same question:
  fork→first  — process start to the first newline the shape prints. For pi that is the
                first correlated RPC response (leg 1, a different question); for the
                candidate CLI it is the version string, or for `rho run` the first JSONL
                record — a session header, so it says the CLI is up and has started the
                turn, not that a model answered. The wall column is the whole turn.
  peak RSS    — VmHWM, the kernel's own high-water mark for that address space, read from
                /proc while the child lives. `os.wait4`'s rusage is *not* the instrument:
                on this box it reports the spawner's resident set for any child small or
                fast enough that the exec has not cleared it (a `/bin/true` spawned here
                reads ~19 MiB, which is this driver's own Python), so every short-lived
                row would have been inflated. The spread line under each row prints what
                rusage would have said, so the mistake is visible instead of hidden.
The candidate's chart measures column 1 and column 3 of the CLI rows. It is not the row
looprs would pay for; leg 4 is.

