-- engines — N whole runtimes in one process (the other way to get N concurrent passes), one run each, then all shut down and dropped
   n=2: rc=0 host peak RSS 4.4 MiB | added 464 KiB building them (232 KiB each) in 0.1 ms | runs wall 190.2 ms | after shutdown+drop, 1380 KiB still resident and 10 (started at 9) threads
   n=4: rc=0 host peak RSS 4.6 MiB | added 616 KiB building them (154 KiB each) in 0.3 ms | runs wall 385.3 ms | after shutdown+drop, 1400 KiB still resident and 10 (started at 9) threads
   n=8: rc=0 host peak RSS 4.8 MiB | added 880 KiB building them (110 KiB each) in 0.4 ms | runs wall 759.1 ms | after shutdown+drop, 1464 KiB still resident and 10 (started at 9) threads

What each number here is:
  rss_kib / hwm_kib — VmRSS and VmHWM for *this* process, read from /proc/self/status by
                      the process itself. VmHWM is the kernel's high-water mark, so a
                      peak cannot be missed by a sampling interval.
  private_dirty_kib — from /proc/self/smaps_rollup: the resident pages that belong to
                      this address space rather than to shared mappings, which is what
                      a host actually pays when it links something in.
  added               — minus the `floor` row of the same binary: the cost of using the
                      runtime, not the cost of having linked it.
  threads             — /proc/self/task count: an embedded runtime arrives with threads,
                      and a host that builds N of them should know whether they multiply.

The provider in this program is a stub that sleeps and answers, and it is *part of the*
*measurement* rather than overhead: rho-sdk ships no model client, so any embedding host
is writing or vendoring one. Its footprint is inside these numbers on purpose.

