pass denominator: median 2814 s over 89 real passes
  re-derived here from the same session corpus the boundary capture cites; the corpus
  is live, so it is not the same file count as the ADR's row (87 passes, 3095 s); the
  two are quoted separately rather than smoothed into one number.

MEMORY — every shape as host + added, never one absolute against another:
  looprs, booted and idle (leg 2)           39.6 MiB
  + one `pi --mode rpc` child (leg 1)       +121.4 MiB   = 161.0 MiB for today's shape, per pass, +121.4 MiB again for every pass in flight
  + rho runtime, one session, one run       +1.2 MiB   — each rung below is a delta over the rung before it, and all of it is measured against the
    `floor` control: the same binary with rho-sdk linked and nothing constructed
      runtime built                       +0.4 MiB
      + session                          +0.1 MiB
      + first run (a tool call included)  +0.8 MiB
  …of which linking rho-sdk alone costs     2.9 MiB before any runtime exists (that is `floor`'s own RSS, and a host pays it whether or not it builds anything)
  …and a long pass grows in place            9520 KiB resident, up from 2976 KiB, after 40 runs of 64 KiB tool output on one session — about 238 KiB per run held
  by that session. A per-pass child pays none of this because it dies; a host that
  keeps one runtime alive pays it and cannot forget it.
  …and the history is the term               a 256 KiB tool payload costs 2196 KiB across three runs; `ladder`'s empty tool prices none of it, and a beads pass is mostly tool output
  = candidate shape                        40.8 MiB
  difference per pass                      120.2 MiB — and the two rows are not the same kind of number:
  pi's is a separate process's resident set, the candidate's is bytes added to a
  host that was already resident. That is the correction the last capture owed.

  What the added row is and what it is not: it is rho's runtime with a provider and a
  tool written by this spike, because `rho-sdk` ships no model client at all (leg 4's
  dependency list). `bigtool` and `history` price the history a pass grows; nothing
  here prices the tool suite, session store, compactor and permission surface a port
  would wire in — and pi's child-in-full already contains all of those, plus Node. So
  the honest pairing is runtime + host's own work against pi's child-in-full; reading
  the ratio as 'rho is N× smaller than pi' is a scope error these rows are written to
  make impossible.

  The CLI row, kept separate on purpose: `rho --version` peaks at 9.6 MiB and answers in 2 ms; `pi --version` 110.5 MiB / 221 ms; `rho run` with a real turn peaks 27.6 MiB. That is the candidate's own chart's row, and it is not the embedded number: a CLI
  child pays config loading, the provider table and a terminal layer a host already has.

