CONCURRENCY — N at once, and which shape runs out of room first:
  N=2: 240.2 MiB across N pi children, 405 ms to all warm   |   20.0 MiB across N rho CLI children (4 ms)   |   one runtime, N sessions: peak 4.7 MiB, 144.8 ms for N runs (sequential: 288.5 ms)   |   N runtimes in one process: peak 4.4 MiB, +464 KiB
  N=4: 494.8 MiB across N pi children, 593 ms to all warm   |   15.7 MiB across N rho CLI children (8 ms)   |   one runtime, N sessions: peak 5.4 MiB, 145.8 ms for N runs (sequential: 577.7 ms)   |   N runtimes in one process: peak 4.6 MiB, +616 KiB
  N=8: 955.3 MiB across N pi children, 1165 ms to all warm   |   47.0 MiB across N rho CLI children (12 ms)   |   one runtime, N sessions: peak 6.4 MiB, 146.2 ms for N runs (sequential: 1156.5 ms)   |   N runtimes in one process: peak 4.8 MiB, +880 KiB
  Memory ceiling 7038.2 MiB ÷ pi's 121.4 MiB a child = ~57 concurrent passes before this box is out of memory (~28 before half of it is gone), against 6.4 MiB for the same N embedded in one process.
  The runtime shape does not multiply: one process, ~1.4 MiB added by all the concurrent runs together, and the wall time is where the concurrency actually shows up (the row above: N runs together vs
  the same N runs one after another). Memory is not what limits the embedded shape.
  Which degrades first: the child shapes, because N children pay N runtimes. The
  embedded shape's limit is not memory at these numbers at all — it is the session
  rule below.

THE CONCURRENCY RULE — the ticket's fifth question, and the answer is the right shape,
  with one wrinkle:
  `one active Run per Session`, hit the way a loop hits it: refused: SessionBusy
  while a run is in flight (`is_running` = true), and 
  ACCEPTED once it has drained (`is_running` = false). N passes are N
  sessions, so the rule does not collide with looprs' shape; two runs on one
  session is a bug in a host, not a limitation of the engine.
  The wrinkle, measured three ways at two turn latencies (leg 4, `settlerace`; the
  counts are `session_busy_refusals` out of the trials printed on the same row):
    start the next run on the terminal event, still holding the first Run
      8/30 refused, 0 still refused after 20 ms   |   1/60 refused, 0 still refused after 20 ms
    same, but drop the first Run before starting the next
      2/30 refused, 0 still refused after 20 ms   |   1/60 refused, 0 still refused after 20 ms
    drain the first run's stream to the end, then advance
      0/30 refused, 0 still refused after 20 ms   |   0/60 refused, 0 still refused after 20 ms
  Which is the answer, and it is the ADR's answer, arrived at from the other side.
  Advancing on the terminal event is refused some fraction of trials; dropping the
  run first lowers the rate and does not remove it. The session's run slot is
  released asynchronously with respect to the event, and `is_running()` disagrees
  with `start()` in both directions at that moment, so neither the event nor that
  predicate is a gate. Draining the stream to its end is the only shape with zero
  refusals, at both pacings. On pi the loop has to *wait*
  for the stream to close because pi's settle window is ~456 ms (ADR-0010); on this
  engine that window measures ~0 ms (`steptiming`'s `settle_window_ms`) and still
  has to be waited for. So switching engines would not have removed the settle
  discipline, which is a fact ADR-0010's settle row did not know and should carry.

Done.
